      "statusChangeNewStatus": "closed",
      "value": "This is intentional, yes. If you call `SendCustomNetworkEvent` without `NetworkCalling.`, you implicitly call it on `this`. This means the first parameter is simply set to the script you are calling it from. Alternatively, you can also have a reference to a different behaviour, and call `reference.SendCustomNetworkEvent` to use `reference` as the \"first parameter\". The `NetworkCalling.SendCustomNetworkEvent` variant just makes that explicit.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2025-05-22T22:00:34.542Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "1. Use something like `NetworkCalling.SendCustomNetworkEvent(NetworkEventTarget.All, nameof(ReceiveNetworkEvent), /* parameters */);`\n\n2. It throws errors because it doesn't convert `NetworkEventTarget.All` and `nameof(ReceiveNetworkEvent)` correctly.\n\n3. This is fixed by deleting `NetworkCalling.` and use `SendCustomNetworkEvent(NetworkEventTarget.All, nameof(ReceiveNetworkEvent), /* parameters */);` directly.\n\n4. The documentation is wrong, or, this is a bug.\n\n```\n\nTo trigger a network event, use the SendCustomNetworkEvent method available on UdonBehaviour and UdonSharpBehaviour, or make an explicit call via NetworkCalling.SendCustomNetworkEvent. These are identical and only provided for compatibility.\n\n```\n\n--------\n\nEdit: on close inspection, it appears that`NetworkCalling.SendCustomNetworkEvent` requires the behavior `instance` (i.e. IUdonEventReceiver) input as the first argument (like what the equivalent Udon Graph node is doing/expecting) .\n\nHowever, `SendCustomNetworkEvent` (without the \"NetworkCalling.\" prefix) no longer requires this argument. The first argument becomes `NetworkEventTarget.All`, and second argument becomes `nameof( /* the custom event */ )`.\n\nIs this intentional?",
